{
  "difficultyUncleAdjustment": {
    "_info": {
      "comment": "Whether the parent having uncles moves difficulty, across the schedule. Held below the bomb's first period so the bomb cannot contribute, and paired: every interval appears once with an uncle-bearing parent and once without, so each label's two readings are a controlled comparison rather than a single value. Through Defuse the parent's uncles are not an input to difficulty at all and the paired readings must be equal -- that equality is the assertion. From Atlantis EIP-100 makes the numerator 2 rather than 1 when the parent carried uncles, so the pairs separate by exactly one unit of parent/2048 at every interval. The harness reduces the uncle count to whether it is non-zero, so these assert the presence of uncles and not how many.",
      "oracle": "core-geth production, v1.12.21-unstable at commit 4185df450, through that client's own difficulty machinery, and round-trip verified through its own reader rather than only generated: counted at case level, because a run that skips everything also reports ok, and one deliberately wrong value per file produced exactly one failure naming exactly that case. THIS KEY IS NOT THE WHOLE PROVENANCE -- see second-derivation, which records an independent re-derivation of every case from the specifications rather than from any client. Reading this key alone under-reports these files as single-source, and a consumer with the file open did exactly that on 2026-08-29.",
      "oracle-version": "v1.12.21-unstable",
      "second-derivation": "Every case in this file was independently re-derived on 2026-08-24 from the specifications rather than from any client -- the Frontier rule, EIP-2's graduated term and EIP-100's uncle numerator -- and all of them agree. That is a second opinion across a representational difference rather than a second copy of the same computation. The derivation was calibrated with controls that must fire: the 13-second case must separate Frontier from Homestead, and an uncle-bearing parent must move difficulty under EIP-100 while leaving it untouched under Homestead. The bomb terms are NOT exercised here -- see heights-carry-no-bomb-term -- so this file says nothing about ECIP-1010 or ECIP-1041 and does not need to; bomb_pause_and_removal.json carries those and asserts them at their real heights.",
      "heights-carry-no-bomb-term": "Held at block 150,000 at every label, which is below the exponential clause's first period, so no bomb term is added under any rule set and the whole of each expectation is the adjustment rule. That makes this file independent of where ECIP-1010's pause sits -- verified: it scores identically under the rebased model and under mainnet's real activation points. `bomb_pause_and_removal.json` is where the heights are load-bearing.",
      "generated": "2026-08-23",
      "unfillable": {
        "ETC_Thanos": "no fork name in the generator",
        "ETC_FrontierThawing": "scheduled and named, but changes no rule and no state",
        "ETC_DAOWars": "aborted; never activated on any chain",
        "ETC_DAOFork": "Ethereum's irregular state change, declined by this chain",
        "ETC_MESSOn": "chain-selection policy, not a state-transition rule",
        "ETC_MESSOff": "chain-selection policy, not a state-transition rule"
      },
      "wrongBuildMethod": "Scored 2026-08-25 by reimplementing the adjustment rule from its specifications -- Frontier's step, EIP-2's graduated term, EIP-100's uncle numerator -- and then breaking that reimplementation one parameter at a time. No client was patched: the rule is a pure function of the parent's difficulty, the two timestamps and the uncle count, so a wrong implementation can be written directly. The correct reimplementation reproduces all 96 cases here, which makes it a second oracle for this file and not merely a scoring tool.",
      "wrongBuildPairing": "READ THIS FIXTURE AND ITS SIBLING AS A PAIR -- neither is sufficient and the scores prove it rather than assert it. block_interval_adjustment is BLIND to EIP-100's uncle numerator being ignored (96/96, the defect invisible) while uncle_adjustment catches it at 72/96. uncle_adjustment is blind to the Frontier bound moving from 13 to 12, and to the clamp moving from -99 to -100, both of which block_interval_adjustment catches. Every defect tried is caught by at least one of the two, and three of the six are caught by exactly one.",
      "wrongBuildScores": {
        "correct": "96/96",
        "eip100UncleNumeratorIgnored": "72/96 -- this is the defect this fixture exists for, and nothing else in the suite catches it",
        "eip100DivisorLeftAtHomesteads10": "60/96",
        "homesteadDivisorUsesEip100s9": "66/96",
        "frontierBound13to12": "96/96 -- NOT caught here; the sibling's job",
        "boundDivisor2048to1024": "22/96",
        "clampMinus99toMinus100": "96/96 -- NOT caught here; the sibling's job"
      }
    },
